WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.040
Maze’s End is a card that sat in the back of my&nbsp;
trade binder, as well as in the back of my mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.800
for ten years. Simply get ten gates, and then&nbsp;
you win the game—it was always an intriguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.680
card in the way most alternate wincons are, but I&nbsp;
could never find a good reason to build a Maze’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.920
End deck. Every so often I’d come back to it, most&nbsp;
notably to ponder a Child of Alara commander deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:26.760
early last year, but it wasn’t until September&nbsp;
that I finally found a good vehicle for it. See,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.720
the issue with Maze’s End is that it’s both&nbsp;
visible and slow—opponents can track the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.080
progress of the deck to its wincon with pinpoint&nbsp;
accuracy, and the timeframe to arrive at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.560
wincon leaves a long period where opponents&nbsp;
can apply pressure. In addition, the 10 gate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.400
requirement means that deckbuilders are encouraged&nbsp;
to play a sequence of pretty mediocre taplands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.800
or else need to activate Maze’s End a large number&nbsp;
of times. However, there is a way to get around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.400
all these problems—what if I simply played all the&nbsp;
gates at once? Cards like Scapeshift and Reshape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.080
the Earth allow deckbuilders to do just this,&nbsp;
getting all the necessary gates for Maze’s End&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.720
out all at once. This can leave a full turn cycle&nbsp;
before victory due to the lands entering tapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.120
unless a player has an Amulet of Vigor in&nbsp;
play, or rather, an untapped turtle friend.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.720
Causing all other permanents to enter either&nbsp;
tapped or untapped depending on his own status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.240
Archelos is a fantastic vehicle for this sort&nbsp;
of deck because not only is he a combo enabler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.640
he can also help out with ramping faster as well&nbsp;
as slowing down opponents. In addition to running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.000
12 gates, the deck ended up running several bounce&nbsp;
lands to give a mana boost with the commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.120
some basics to be grabbed with land-grabbing&nbsp;
spells, and a handful of other utility lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:41.400
for a grand total of 43, not including Maze’s&nbsp;
End. Of the two cards that allow the deck to win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.840
one costs nine mana and the other requires ten&nbsp;
lands, so naturally ramp ends up being a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.480
big part of this deck. There are 20 dedicated&nbsp;
ramp spells as well as an additional four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.920
ramp spells that serve double-duty by also&nbsp;
offering a way to tap down the commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.000
and being able to force everyone’s stuff to&nbsp;
enter tapped until my next turn is a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.320
sweet side benefit. From there, the deck mostly&nbsp;
consists of draw, tutors, and interaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.960
The most potent of the deck’s tutors is&nbsp;
Emergent Ultimatum, a card that basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.880
serves as an additional combo piece, since any&nbsp;
combination of Reshape the Earth, Scapeshift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.840
and Boundless Realms will result in getting&nbsp;
enough lands to win. As far as interaction goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.120
I always like to have a lot of counterspells&nbsp;
in my blue combo decks, so this deck runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.240
eleven of those and six other cards intended to&nbsp;
control the state of the game in some capacity.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.240
The deck took a lot of tweaking to get to the&nbsp;
point where it functioned appropriately—how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.440
much ramp? How much draw? What sort of lands? How&nbsp;
should the deck go about surviving and preventing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.040
opponents from winning? How important is the&nbsp;
commander, and how much should I be leveraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.960
him for non-combo purposes? I spent several&nbsp;
weeks and dozens of hours tweaking and tuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.560
and pruning and answering tough questions until&nbsp;
I’d achieved the seemingly impossible—a deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.880
could pull off a Maze’s End win consistently by&nbsp;
turn 7 or 8 with plenty of interaction held up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.960
all for a bit of over a hundred bucks. It was&nbsp;
an accomplishment, a realization of my past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:06.840
ambitions, an optimized utilization of a silly&nbsp;
alternate wincon card I’d been thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:13.040
for ten whole years. And, if the story ended&nbsp;
here, it would be an unambiguously happy one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.440
an uncomplicated tale of self-actualization&nbsp;
and cardboard triumph. However, it was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.800
fact somewhat complicated by a simple&nbsp;
fact—Magic the Gathering is a card game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.880
and decks are meant to be played in games&nbsp;
against people. In my first few games with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.160
deck I was kind of surprised with the way people&nbsp;
responded to me pulling off my massive land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.880
swing—I’d get excited and start grabbing gates,&nbsp;
and other people would basically just go “oh you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:46.360
got the combo? Okay cool, another game?” I had&nbsp;
constructed a grand narrative for this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:51.920
because it was genuinely challenging to build,&nbsp;
but in an actual game of commander this plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.920
just like any other 1 card + commander&nbsp;
combo deck. Satisfy conditions, get pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.560
stop opponents from winning long enough to put&nbsp;
things together, protect combo, and do the combo.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.440
It’s not necessarily that the deck was&nbsp;
entirely uninteresting or entirely devoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:11.600
of fun gameplay—it’s an acceptable combo deck. The&nbsp;
real surprise was the expectation vs the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.080
of the situation—I had constructed narratives&nbsp;
of grandeur as I was reaching out, striving for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:22.080
a powerful Maze’s End deck, but once it was&nbsp;
in my hand it was clearly something far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.440
mundane than I had expected. And, on some level&nbsp;
it’s a little strange that this happened to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.760
because I had watched my friend Hans have a very&nbsp;
similar experience a few months prior building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.480
Yidris deck. This was a deck that used Yidris’&nbsp;
unique combat damage ability to cascade into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:44.200
an “each opponent discards their hand, you draw 28&nbsp;
cards” combo off of two cantrips. It was otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.720
built in a midrange shell, containing ramp,&nbsp;
removal, value engines, and some very specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.360
protection spells that were chosen in order to&nbsp;
still get value when they were cascaded into. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.680
what was the problem there? Well once&nbsp;
again, despite having a creative execution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.480
this deck’s combo was still a combo, and&nbsp;
opponents treated it as such. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:05.040
this deck was even more problematic than the&nbsp;
Archelos deck due to the fact that all of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.200
shenanigans could be stopped by simply killing&nbsp;
or fully blocking the Yidris, and since not doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.880
that meant possibly ceding a massive value swing&nbsp;
to the Yidris player, players would religiously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.913
hate out Yidris. On paper, everything lined up&nbsp;
for the deck, but real life is not made of paper.

00:05:20.913 --> 00:05:21.038
MTG AS A TWO-PHASE GAME

00:05:21.038 --> 00:05:26.160
A question I’ve asked myself is, what are these&nbsp;
decks saying exactly? They say something about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.000
combos, and about humans, and about how&nbsp;
we build decks, but that’s not the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.400
interesting thing they say. The most interesting&nbsp;
thing these goofy combo decks tell me is that deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.240
building creativity is not rewarded in and of&nbsp;
itself in games of commander. This might sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.480
like the whining of some hipster, but I’m not&nbsp;
intending that statement as any sort of value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:47.520
judgment about the state of the game—rather,&nbsp;
it provides an interesting jumping off point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.240
for thinking more critically about the game and&nbsp;
how it’s experienced by the people who play it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.680
An inherent weirdness of a collectible card game&nbsp;
like Magic: the Gathering is that deckbuilding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:01.480
and playing are two separate phases of the game.&nbsp;
You’re not playing Dominion or some other card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:06.520
game where deckbuilding and playing happen in&nbsp;
tandem; no, these things are clearly demarcated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.520
Limited formats provide the most conventionally&nbsp;
game-like structure for these phases, in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:15.840
that a four or five hour limited event literally&nbsp;
contains separate timed phases for assembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.920
decks and playing the game. A Magic: the Gathering&nbsp;
draft, where players pick cards from packs in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:26.040
circle and build decks with their resulting card&nbsp;
pool before entering a series of timed rounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:30.400
actually resembles a lot of board and card&nbsp;
games in structure. As a simple example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.120
For Sale is a game that contains one phase where&nbsp;
players bid against each other on property cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.920
and then a second phase where players use their&nbsp;
lineup of properties to secure point cards. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.960
a Magic: the Gathering draft, the first phase&nbsp;
involves a mixture of strategy and luck to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:48.400
determine how good a player’s group of cards&nbsp;
is, and then the second phase uses the quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.840
of that group as well as some further strategy&nbsp;
and luck to determine the ultimate outcome of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:57.360
the game. This is a clear enough line to draw&nbsp;
with limited Magic: the Gathering events, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.640
the relationship between these different phases&nbsp;
gets progressively more convoluted from here.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.880
Let’s think about a low-level Standard event.&nbsp;
In the deckbuilding phase, players on their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:10.040
time and in their own spaces complete the task&nbsp;
of assembling the best standard deck they can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:13.800
and the best sideboard to face off against&nbsp;
the decks they expect to face. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.320
the playing phase sees the players, now&nbsp;
congregating in a game store at a specific time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.000
play a sequence of games to determine a winner.&nbsp;
This is very different from most games in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.880
different phases occur in different settings on&nbsp;
different timelines, but the structure is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.080
roughly the same. The first phase sees players&nbsp;
complete one goal, which then contributes to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.080
outcome of a second goal. However, taking things&nbsp;
a step further into the realm of commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.640
things get even murkier, because you lose&nbsp;
a lot of the element of competitiveness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.680
The first phase does still involve building a&nbsp;
deck, and the second phase does still involve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:49.040
playing that deck, but the goals are much&nbsp;
less clear. Unlike a standard tournament,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.480
there usually isn’t a prize for winning&nbsp;
an EDH game aside from bragging rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:56.360
and even in store-run casual commander events&nbsp;
the subject of prize support is one with no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:01.680
shortage of disagreement and controversy. Put&nbsp;
simply, winning isn’t codified as a goal in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.960
same way as it is in 60 card constructed events.&nbsp;
This difference in how winning is perceived then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.520
trickles back down to the deckbuilding phase. If&nbsp;
everybody was sincerely trying their best to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.960
the most powerful deck in that deckbuilding phase,&nbsp;
commander games would be full of netdecked cEDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:20.600
lists, but most players choose to show up to EDH&nbsp;
games with decks that look quite different than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:25.520
that. Winning will be a contributing factor to the&nbsp;
sorts of decks that EDH players will build, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:29.680
will be accompanied by a melange of other factors&nbsp;
rendered subtle in competitive 1v1 formats—wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:33.680
to do a certain cool play, wanting&nbsp;
to have a deck feel a certain way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.440
wanting a deck to look a certain way, wanting a&nbsp;
deck to produce the funniest moments, and so on.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:42.680
A lot of these goals will mesh well with the&nbsp;
task of having fun in that second phase—obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.920
players playing decks that feel expressive or&nbsp;
cool to them in some way will usually lead to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:53.040
them having more fun, and the goal of winning will&nbsp;
likewise produce a lot of fun moments for people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.480
However, there are also other goals that don’t&nbsp;
mesh as well with having a fun game of commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:03.520
and the best example of these is decks that try to&nbsp;
win in some especially creative or expressive way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.280
As it turns out, winning the game is generally&nbsp;
a pretty brief process. A long period of back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:13.040
and forth can be fun, but when a game reaches a&nbsp;
point where one person is all but guaranteed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:19.800
win it’s generally for the best that things stop&nbsp;
there. This is also why nondeterministic combo or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.240
lockout decks are often unpopular—they stretch&nbsp;
out the length of a win, requiring opponents to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.000
make an unfortunate choice: either concede to&nbsp;
save time, or waste an extra 10 minutes on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:34.760
5% chance that the comboing player fizzles. But&nbsp;
okay, I’m getting off topic. The platonic ideal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:40.320
of an EDH win is brief—there is no ceremony where&nbsp;
the winning player gets to revel in their glory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.440
and be celebrated for their deckbuilding or&nbsp;
gameplay accomplishments, the game just ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:48.440
and people move on to either another game or&nbsp;
any of the other things that might occupy their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:54.120
life. Winning in that creative way might be cool&nbsp;
for that first time or two, but maybe not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:59.400
that—the really memorable games are usually the&nbsp;
ones where something unexpected happens, not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:04.257
ones where a player trots out their creatively&nbsp;
devised wincon they spent hours creating.

00:10:04.257 --> 00:10:04.280
THE FIEND HUNTING PHASE

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:09.160
But, spending hours to creatively devise and&nbsp;
perfect a wincon still has an allure to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:14.200
doesn’t it? Despite understanding all of what I&nbsp;
just said, I have a pattern of continually coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:20.000
back to build new creative combo decks—it’s&nbsp;
an itch that I can’t seem to stop scratching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.440
Another one I built in paper a few months back was&nbsp;
one I like to call Tribal Fiend Hunter. The basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:28.720
idea behind it was this—you know Fiend Hunters,&nbsp;
those creatures that exile something else until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:33.120
they leave play? Well, what if you built a deck&nbsp;
with the goal of getting three of them and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.080
having them exile each other repeatedly in an&nbsp;
indefinitely long loop? Just a few fellas having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:43.520
a really phenomenal day of fiend hunting. This&nbsp;
is a somewhat unique spin on a combo deck—instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.480
of finding a handful of different pieces in&nbsp;
separate categories, you just need to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:52.240
three of the eight Fiend Hunters that exist within&nbsp;
the green white color identity. The deck then wins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:57.240
through some means or other—a couple of the Fiend&nbsp;
Hunters also create tokens or counters, and three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:02.200
of them exile multiple cards, which allows for&nbsp;
infinite ETB triggers. Speaking of ETB triggers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:07.400
I went with the commander choice of Torsten,&nbsp;
Founder of Benalia. Torsten has a lot of benefits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:11.600
with the biggest ones being that he draws a bunch&nbsp;
of cards on ETB, a fact which can be paired with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:16.320
cards that flicker in order to draw the number of&nbsp;
cards that the deck needs to draw in order to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:22.080
three Fiend Hunters—37 on average. So, the deck&nbsp;
began with a mixture of Fiend Hunters and sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:28.800
of blink. From there, most of the deck ended up&nbsp;
occupied by lands and ramp creatures—42 and 29,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:34.280
respectively. This might seem like a lot,&nbsp;
because it is—I have a 7 mana commander to cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:38.520
and since he’s the only source of draw in&nbsp;
a deck that wants to draw a ton of cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:43.440
the deck doesn’t really do anything before he gets&nbsp;
cast. The mana dorks of the deck are carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.240
selected to offer the most utility during the&nbsp;
Fiend Hunting combo step—there are 6 different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:52.440
ones that can give infinite mana, infinite&nbsp;
+1/+1 counters, or some other resource that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:56.880
can hopefully lead to winning, and the deck’s&nbsp;
removal likewise fits into this mold. Overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:01.280
the goal here was to have a combo deck that&nbsp;
is as fluid and flexible as possible, using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.120
the power of Fiend Hunters to creatively piece&nbsp;
together wins in a variety of different ways.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:09.840
Initially I considered including some pricier&nbsp;
cards in the deck like Preston the Vanisher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.920
or even switching the commander to Atraxa&nbsp;
for a four-color version, but eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:19.240
I came to the conclusion that I did not want to&nbsp;
spend more than $50 on this deck. The logic was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:24.680
this—I knew that I found the Fiend Hunter combo&nbsp;
funny, but I had been down the funny combo road&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:29.320
before and the resulting deck didn’t end up fully&nbsp;
feeling worth the money I spent on it. With that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:33.760
in mind I figured there would be two reasonably&nbsp;
likely possibilities—either this deck ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:38.040
up being a hilarious blast to play and I’d keep&nbsp;
finding reasons to pull it out, a situation worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:43.320
well more than $50 to me, or it’d end up being a&nbsp;
novelty that I’d get a handful of funny games out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:50.120
of before more or less being shelved, a situation&nbsp;
worth around $50. Sure enough, the reality of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:54.600
situation was far closer to the latter. The deck&nbsp;
functioned pretty amusingly once it was up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:59.600
going, and it would typically combo off around&nbsp;
turn 7 which isn’t half bad for a $50 deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:04.120
but this came with a catch. The 7 mana&nbsp;
commander and the fact that all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:08.560
ramp was creature-based meant that the deck&nbsp;
was extremely disruptible—killing a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:12.120
mana dorks in the first few turns of the game&nbsp;
would immediately set the deck back several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:17.800
turns. This deck was carefully crafted with just&nbsp;
barely enough of any given resource to function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:22.440
and this ended up being its biggest weakness—the&nbsp;
deck just has too many possible ways to kneecap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:27.320
it to keep up in most high power casual&nbsp;
games. The deck wasn’t a total failure—it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.280
was a success of budget deckbuilding, and a&nbsp;
success of my own ability to show restraint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:35.560
and know when I shouldn’t spend too much on a&nbsp;
deck. However, it was still yet another goofy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:39.640
combo deck that didn’t really succeed at&nbsp;
being a deck I’d want to play long term.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:43.640
The Archelos deck and the Fiend Hunter deck are&nbsp;
the only two I’ve built in paper, but there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:47.960
several other goofy combo decks I’ve workshopped&nbsp;
and considered online decklists for over the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:51.760
several months. There was that polymorph deck&nbsp;
whose only three creatures were Trench Gorger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.640
Balustrade Spy, and Timestream Navigator, with&nbsp;
the goal being to mill my entire library before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:59.920
taking infinite turns. There was that Brinelin&nbsp;
Dargo deck that had the goal of casting Dargo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:04.880
a large number of times before doing—something, I&nbsp;
honestly don’t remember what at this point. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:08.800
was also that time a patron’s deck sent me down&nbsp;
a rabbit hole of trying to build Rograkh Storm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:12.680
a deck with the goal of casting Rograkh an&nbsp;
arbitrarily large number of times before Jeska-ing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:17.040
my opponents for some absurd amount. I’ve had&nbsp;
enough sense to not build any of these three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:23.240
but still—why do I keep finding myself in&nbsp;
this place again and again? Fundamentally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:27.760
I think it comes down to looking for a sense of&nbsp;
accomplishment. Let’s go back to the two phases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:32.080
model of Magic: the Gathering gameplay. Unlike a&nbsp;
60 card constructed tournament where winning is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:36.840
the #1 goal during the playing stage of things,&nbsp;
outside of cEDH there can be any number of goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:41.040
during this phase of a commander game, each&nbsp;
holding varying levels of priority. Winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.720
will be one for most players, but beyond that&nbsp;
players kind of need to pick out what they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:49.480
looking for from a game. Are they trying to have a&nbsp;
fun, social experience? Are they trying to impress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:53.880
other players, to do a cool thing? Are they&nbsp;
trying to mimic a game of Uno? Are they trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:57.600
assemble the largest collection of women looking&nbsp;
left in the Western Hemisphere? Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:01.480
players end up creating their own goals for the&nbsp;
playing phase of games, either consciously or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:06.545
unconsciously, and these goals will then serve as&nbsp;
a guiding force during their deckbuilding phase.96

00:15:06.545 --> 00:15:06.560
THE WONK FACTOR

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:11.200
An underrated factor that I’d guess affects almost&nbsp;
all players in this format is aimlessness—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:14.520
human brain goes a little wonky when it doesn’t&nbsp;
have enough structure to guide it through any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:18.560
given task at hand, and for a lot of players the&nbsp;
amount of structure that the commander format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:23.720
provides is an amount that puts them in that wonk&nbsp;
zone. It’s not hard to figure out why—the format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:27.720
contains almost every card ever printed, and&nbsp;
doesn’t have set in stone parameters for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:32.240
decks should do or how powerful they should be.&nbsp;
For newer players in the format building with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:36.120
they have on hand, trying to build the strongest&nbsp;
deck possible will likely keep them occupied for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:39.880
awhile, and this is indeed quite often to see with&nbsp;
players who have just discovered the format and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:43.760
are looking to explore the limits of what they&nbsp;
can do with it. This is because player skill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:47.200
budgetary restrictions, and card pool&nbsp;
restrictions can serve as limitations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:51.400
things that a player will be optimizing against&nbsp;
whether they’re aware of it or not. Even after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.360
players have gotten to the point where they&nbsp;
are capable of building more powerful decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:58.480
than they could previously, they will oftentimes&nbsp;
still seek out limitations in some form or other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:02.720
such as budget, aesthetic, mechanical,&nbsp;
or otherwise. These sorts of limitations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:07.040
can be quite helpful for maintaining a focus&nbsp;
while deckbuilding—I frequently build with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.800
budget restriction of some sort, as this has&nbsp;
dual benefits of preventing me from spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.760
more than I want to as well as giving me&nbsp;
a parameter to optimize against. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:19.080
an extra too many words to say about the subject&nbsp;
of constrained optimization as applied to Magic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:23.040
the Gathering that would derail this video with&nbsp;
a torrent of tangentially-related mathy bullshit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:26.600
so that stuff is going to be in an&nbsp;
extra posted over on my patreon.

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:30.160
Long story short, constraints can provide a&nbsp;
nice guiding hand in the deckbuilding process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:34.800
and they can present goals that allow players to&nbsp;
have more fun while building decks. However, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:39.560
will often be less felt within a game. Building&nbsp;
a Volo deck you might choose to have all your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:44.040
creatures be different types, and that might pose&nbsp;
a fun challenge during the deckbuilding process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.520
but once you’re in an actual game, odds are&nbsp;
you’re just gonna be playing generic snowbally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:52.640
midrange. Likewise, building decks with the goal&nbsp;
of accomplishing a cool or unique play pattern can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:57.440
be a fun puzzle to solve during the deckbuilding&nbsp;
phase, but once you’re playing actual games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:02.960
that cool plan won’t necessarily translate into&nbsp;
an interesting game. So what does translate into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:07.440
an interesting game? Well, when I look at all&nbsp;
of my decks, there are two decks that stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:13.400
out as being the most consistently fun ones—my&nbsp;
Radha deck, a greedy cascade battlecruiser deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:18.600
and my Sharuum Slide deck, a cycling-based&nbsp;
toolbox control deck. These two decks share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:23.480
three key attributes—consistency,&nbsp;
flexibility, and resiliency.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:28.320
By consistency I simply mean that these decks&nbsp;
always do something—they don’t generally have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:33.520
bad games and they are always doing something to&nbsp;
further their gameplan. By flexibility I mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:37.720
what precisely these decks are doing can vary&nbsp;
a lot and can be shaped to the game they happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:42.920
to end up in. Though both decks lean toward late&nbsp;
game domination, they also both have ways to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:47.240
on some pressure if there’s another deck that&nbsp;
is at risk of getting too far ahead. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:52.240
by resiliency I mean that these decks are&nbsp;
hard to fully shut down. My Radha deck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:57.080
iconically resilient due to its focus on basic&nbsp;
lands, a seldom-hated resource—nothing short of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:01.040
reducing this deck’s life total to zero will&nbsp;
really shut it down. The Sharuum Slide deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.880
is somewhat more hateable, but it is still&nbsp;
fairly resilient due to how many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:08.760
ways it has of building resources—it has a&nbsp;
graveyard plan, it has an artifacts plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:12.240
it has an Astral Slide ETB flicker plan,&nbsp;
it has a flying creature beatdown plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:16.200
and it has a hand-sculpting control plan—there&nbsp;
are just so many different elements working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:20.360
tandem that if one or two get knocked out the&nbsp;
deck can simply pick a different direction to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:24.840
build advantage in. You may find different&nbsp;
deck attributes to be enjoyable than I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:28.120
but these are just the three that have&nbsp;
proved most consistently fun for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:31.280
and they’re a good starting place if&nbsp;
you’re not yet sure what you find fun.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:34.960
A final additional element that these decks&nbsp;
share is that their engines and interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:38.960
suite are well-defined but their wincons are&nbsp;
less well-defined, because they’re both late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:43.200
game decks—the Radha deck is looking to build&nbsp;
up an absurd land count and then win, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.840
Sharuum Slide deck is a control deck that wants to&nbsp;
gain a degree of dominance over the state of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:52.880
game before it finishes off opponents. To me, this&nbsp;
goes back to the length of wincons—because these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:56.960
decks have states they’re trying to achieve&nbsp;
rather than discrete plans to win the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:01.600
winning games is more of an involved process&nbsp;
than it is in most of my other decks. Whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:05.720
with a lot of decks the “win” is quite rapid once&nbsp;
the deck is in a good position, with a slower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:11.240
more controlling deck there will be more time to&nbsp;
simmer in the win. That’s certainly more enjoyable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:16.440
for me while playing commander, and I think it’s&nbsp;
useful to identify that, but I hesitate to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:21.760
it an unambiguous good—is it good for the table&nbsp;
if I always pull out this sort of grindy endgame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:27.200
deck? Is it good for me to always pick this sort&nbsp;
of fun-optimized play experience for myself?

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:31.600
At the end of the day, there is a valid place for&nbsp;
novelty decks out there. In spite of everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:36.240
I’m ultimately glad I built both my Turtle Gates&nbsp;
deck and my Fiend Hunter deck—they were fun to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:39.840
build, and it’s nice to have a bit of extra&nbsp;
variety around if I’m ever starting to bore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:45.400
of my first or second string decks. It’s valid to&nbsp;
have different decks that serve different desires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:49.800
and not every deck needs to be some sort of&nbsp;
optimal construct of mathematically maximized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:54.480
fun value. It’s even valid to build a deck&nbsp;
primarily for the enjoyment of building it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:59.000
and whether or not it ends up materializing in&nbsp;
paper can be a matter of personal preference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:03.200
What I would advocate for, however, is&nbsp;
mindfulness. When you’re building a deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:07.280
it’s good to calibrate your expectations and&nbsp;
understand what that deck is going to mean for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:12.080
you. Get to know yourself—what do you find fun?&nbsp;
What goals do you strive for in the gameplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:16.360
stage of things, and how can you contribute to&nbsp;
those goals in the deckbuilding stage? Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:20.560
worthwhile to introduce constraints, and how will&nbsp;
those constraints affect the gameplay portion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:25.000
things as compared to the deckbuilding portion?&nbsp;
Because ultimately, a holistic understanding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:30.640
the game needs to include both of these things.&nbsp;
Deckbuilding is playing Magic on some level—it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:35.480
just playing Magic in a different way than&nbsp;
the games themselves, and to different ends.

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:40.680
Thanks for watching! This was an expansion on a&nbsp;
Patron-only video I released seven months ago—I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:44.520
finally felt like I had enough to say to turn&nbsp;
it into a full-length video. If you wanna see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:48.600
me chew on ideas weeks or months before&nbsp;
they actually end up in a public video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.720
you can support me over on Patreon. My&nbsp;
lovely Patrons, some of whom are on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:57.320
now, make it possible for me to take my time on&nbsp;
longer videos like this one, and without even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:02.000
needing to include midroll ads. Beyond that, if&nbsp;
you’re feeling inspired to buy some funny combo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:08.520
cards or some non-funny non-combo cards, I left a&nbsp;
TCGPlayer affiliate link down in the description.

